   "source": [
    "## Setup: upload `week5_kigali_weather.xlsx` 
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 1 · List Comprehensions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "temps = [24.4, 25.9, 28.6, 19.3, 26.0, 22.1]\n",
    "raw = [\"24.4\", \"25.9\", \"28.6\", \"19.3\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Ex 1.1: comprehension converts each temperature to Fahrenheit (t*9/5+32).\n",
    "temps_f = [t * 9/5 + 32 for t in temps]\n",
    "print(temps_f)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 1.2: (a) filter with \"if t >= 26\"; (b) filter \"t < 24\" and round each value.\n",
    "hot = [t for t in temps if t >= 26]\n",
    "cool_rounded = [round(t) for t in temps if t < 24]\n",
    "print(hot)\n",
    "print(cool_rounded)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 1.3: float(s) converts each CSV string to a number, then sum/len gives the average.\n",
    "nums = [float(s) for s in raw]\n",
    "print(nums)\n",
    "print(sum(nums) / len(nums))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 2 · lambda"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 2.1: sorted with key=lambda r: r[1] and reverse=True ranks by score; [:3] is the top 3.\n",
    "results = [(\"Aline\", 85), (\"Eric\", 78), (\"Diane\", 91), (\"Samuel\", 92), (\"Grace\", 76)]\n",
    "ranked = sorted(results, key=lambda r: r[1], reverse=True)\n",
    "print(ranked[:3])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 2.2: max with key=lambda p: p[1] finds the (day, rainfall) pair with the most rain.\n",
    "rain_log = [(3, 0.0), (7, 12.5), (11, 3.2), (14, 26.2), (18, 8.9)]\n",
    "wettest = max(rain_log, key=lambda p: p[1])\n",
    "print(wettest)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 3 · NumPy Arrays"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Loading cell: reads the Excel file and builds dates, temps, rain, hum.\n",
    "# Dates in Excel are stored as serial numbers, so they are converted to real dates here.\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_excel(\"week5_kigali_weather.xlsx\")\n",
    "\n",
    "d = df[\"date\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "if np.issubdtype(d.dtype, np.number):\n",
    "    dates = (np.datetime64(\"1899-12-30\") + d.astype(int).astype(\"timedelta64[D]\")).astype(str)\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    dates = pd.to_datetime(d).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\").to_numpy().astype(str)\n",
    "\n",
    "temps = df[\"temp_c\"].to_numpy(dtype=float)\n",
    "rain  = df[\"rainfall_mm\"].to_numpy(dtype=float)\n",
    "hum   = df[\"humidity_pct\"].to_numpy(dtype=float)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(len(temps), temps[:5])\n",
    "print(dates[:3])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 3.1: speed test, Python loop vs NumPy on one million numbers.\n",
    "import time\n",
    "n = 1_000_000\n",
    "\n",
    "data = list(range(n))\n",
    "start = time.time()\n",
    "result = [x * 2 for x in data]\n",
    "loop_time = time.time() - start\n",
    "\n",
    "arr = np.arange(n)\n",
    "start = time.time()\n",
    "result = arr * 2\n",
    "numpy_time = time.time() - start\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Python: {loop_time:.4f}s\")\n",
    "print(f\"NumPy:  {numpy_time:.4f}s\")\n",
    "print(f\"NumPy was about {loop_time / numpy_time:.0f}x faster\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# NumPy was roughly 4 times faster on my machine (see the printed result above)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 3.2: array methods give mean, max, min, std. Checkpoint: mean = 24.00 °C.\n",
    "print(f\"Mean: {temps.mean():.2f} °C\")\n",
    "print(f\"Max:  {temps.max():.2f} °C\")\n",
    "print(f\"Min:  {temps.min():.2f} °C\")\n",
    "print(f\"Std:  {temps.std():.2f} °C\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 3.3: np.argmax gives the position of the maximum; dates[pos] gives the date.\n",
    "pos = np.argmax(temps)\n",
    "print(f\"Hottest day: {dates[pos]} at {temps[pos]} °C\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 4 · Boolean Masking"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 4.1: mask temps >= 26. .sum() counts the hot days; temps[mask].mean() averages them.\n",
    "hot_mask = temps >= 26\n",
    "print(f\"Hot days: {hot_mask.sum()}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Average on hot days: {temps[hot_mask].mean():.2f} °C\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 4.2: mask rain > 0 counts rainy days; rain.sum() is the total; rain.max() the heaviest day.\n",
    "rainy_mask = rain > 0\n",
    "print(f\"Rainy days: {rainy_mask.sum()}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Total rainfall: {rain.sum():.1f} mm\")\n",
    "print(f\"Heaviest day: {rain.max():.1f} mm\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 4.3: cross-array analysis. Rainy days avg 24.14 °C vs dry 23.95 °C (almost no difference).\n",
    "# Hot AND rainy uses & with parentheses around each condition.\n",
    "print(f\"Rainy days avg temp: {temps[rain > 0].mean():.2f} °C\")\n",
    "print(f\"Dry days avg temp:   {temps[rain == 0].mean():.2f} °C\")\n",
    "\n",
    "hot_and_rainy = (temps >= 26) & (rain > 0)\n",
    "print(f\"Hot and rainy days: {hot_and_rainy.sum()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Humid days (>= 70%): {(hum >= 70).sum()}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 5 · Anomalies & Trends"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 5.1: anomaly = temps - mean, vectorized. Mask picks dates from the dates array.\n",
    "anomaly = temps - temps.mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "hot_anomalies = anomaly > 3\n",
    "print(\"Days more than 3 °C above mean:\")\n",
    "print(dates[hot_anomalies])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Days more than 3 °C below mean: {(anomaly < -3).sum()}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Ex 5.2: compare the first 30 days with the last 30 days.\n",
    "first_half = temps[:30].mean()\n",
    "last_half = temps[30:].mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"First 30 days avg: {first_half:.2f} °C\")\n",
    "print(f\"Last 30 days avg:  {last_half:.2f} °C\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Kigali got cooler in the last 30 days (about 4.4 °C lower on average)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Bonus · Top 5 wettest days"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Bonus: zip pairs dates with rain; sorted with a lambda ranks them (loop only for printing).\n",
    "pairs = list(zip(dates, rain))\n",
    "top5 = sorted(pairs, key=lambda p: p[1], reverse=True)[:5]\n",
    "\n",
    "for date, mm in top5:\n",
    "    print(f\"{date}: {mm} mm\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Extra for the report: wettest day details and temperature-rain correlation.\n",
    "pos = np.argmax(rain)\n",
    "print(dates[pos], rain[pos], temps[pos])\n",
    "print(np.corrcoef(temps, rain)[0, 1])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 6 · Reflection\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. What did NumPy hide, and why was the loop version still important?**\n",
    "\n",
    "NumPy hid the running total, the counter, and the step-by-step pass through the data that I wrote by hand in Week 3. With `temps.mean()` it all happens in one call, and it runs in fast compiled code, which is why it beat the Python loop in the speed test. Learning the loop first was still important because it showed me what \"average\" really does, so I can understand the result and debug it when something is wrong. For example, my dates came out as Excel numbers like 46272, and I could only fix that because I understood what the data was before NumPy processed it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. One insight about Kigali's weather**\n",
    "\n",
    "Rain did not cool Kigali in this period. Rainy days averaged 24.14 °C and dry days 23.95 °C, a difference of less than 0.2 °C. The correlation between temperature and rainfall was about 0.09, and the wettest day (7 September, 26.2 mm) was also one of the hottest at 27.6 °C. The real change was seasonal: the first 30 days averaged 26.21 °C and the last 30 averaged 21.78 °C, about 4.4 °C cooler. This covers only 60 days of one season, so it describes this period and not Kigali's climate in general."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Short Report\n",
    "\n",
    "I analysed 60 days of Kigali weather data (September to October 2026) using Python and NumPy, with no loops in Parts 3 to 5.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key insight: rain did not cool Kigali in this period.** Rainy days averaged 24.14 °C and dry days 23.95 °C. The correlation between temperature and rainfall was about 0.09, close to zero. The wettest day, 7 September (26.2 mm), was also one of the hottest at 27.6 °C.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What did change was the season.** The first 30 days averaged 26.21 °C and the last 30 averaged 21.78 °C, about 4.4 °C cooler. There were 18 hot days (≥ 26 °C, average 27.11 °C) and 16 rainy days, with 331.2 mm of total rainfall. The hottest day was 17 September at 28.6 °C.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limits:** this is only 60 days from one season, so the results describe this period and say nothing about Kigali's long-term climate."
   ]
  }
